{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01GnhYr58jBTm8MdfRDVuw7h","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# 2DPartInt\n2\t\n3\tGeotechnical problems have been traditionally tackled as an extension of continuum mechanics theories. Such an approach, although appropriate for systems undergoing small displacements, cannot examine important problems such as landslides, rockfalls, and rainfall-induced landslides. Our implementation is a first step toward analyzing these complex behavior, and, as a starting point, studies systems constituted of geomaterials assumed as particles in a two-dimensional space. With this implementation, we plan to bring to the geotechnical community, academic and practitioners in general, a computational tool that will allow solving challenges involving multiphysics and large displacements effectively and accurately.\n4\t\n5\t![Simulation](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/eafit-apolo/2DPartInt/master/simulacion.gif)\n6\t\n7\t## Usage\n8\t\n9\tCompile the project using make.\n10\t\n11\t```bash\n12\t$ make\n13\t```\n14\t\n15\t_(Optional)_ run the tests to ensure everything is ok.\n16\t\n17\t\n18\t```bash\n19\t$ make test\n20\t```\n21\t\n22\tTo debug some simulation step compile like this.\n23\t\n24\t```bash\n25\t$ make DEBUG_STEP=true\n26\t```\n27\t\n28\tExecute the program passing the `simulation_config.txt` file & the output folder as the arguments.\n29\t\n30\t```bash\n31\t$ ./bin/2DPartInt simulation_config.txt out/\n32\t```\n33\t\n34\t### Profile the program\n35\t\n36\tInstall the package `gperftools` or the equivalent in your operating system.\n37\t\n38\tCompile defining the `PROFILING` flag.\n39\t\n40\t```\n41\t$ make PROFILING=true\n42\t```\n43\t\n44\tRun defining where to store the profiler output.\n45\t\n46\t```\n47\t$ CPUPROFILE=profiler_out.prof ./bin/2DPartInt simulation_config.txt out/\n48\t```\n49\t\n50\tPrint profiling information.\n51\t\n52\t```\n53\t$ pprof --text ./bin/2DPartInt profiler_out.prof\n54\t```\n55\t\n56\tMore runtime and offline options for the profiler [here](https://gperftools.github.io/gperftools/cpuprofile.html).\n57\t\n58\t## Simulation Config File.\n59\t\n60\tThe behaviour of the simulation is determined by the config file. For finding collisions between particles, we use a Grid-like\n61\tdata structure. The area covered by the Grid will be the only area in space that will be simulated. Particles whose center lies\n62\toutside the Grid area will not be taken into account for findind collisions between particles, even though they would mantain\n63\ttheir forces.\n64\t\n65\tIn the config file one defines the number of squares on the X coordinate and the number of squares on the Y coordinate, along\n66\twith the length of each square. A configuration of x_squares=4, y_squares=3 will yield the following grid:\n67\t\n68\t![OnlyGrid](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/eafit-apolo/2DPartInt/master/img/OnlyGrid.jpg)\n69\t\n70\tThis is the structure of the file.\n71\t_(All settings are mandatory, but they can be in any order). You should not add comentaries as below._\n72\t\n73\t```\n74\ttime=[Double] # Total time to simulate, in seconds.\n75\tdt=[Double] # Size of the time window used for each step.\n76\tx_particles=[Int] # Number of particles along the X coordinate\n77\ty_particles=[Int] # Number of particles along the Y coordinate\n78\tx_squares=[Int] # Number of squares along the X coordinate\n79\ty_squares=[Int] # Number of squares along the X coordinate\n80\tsquare_in_grid_length=[Double] # Length side of each square in the grid\n81\tradius=[Int] # Radius of each particle.\n82\tkn=[Double] # Normal rigidity of the material.\n83\tks=[Double] # Tangential rigidity of the material.\n84\trho=[Double] # Density of the material.\n85\tthickness=[Double] # Thickness of the particles.\n86\tv0=[Double] # Initial velocity of the falling particle.\n87\tr0=[Double] # Radius of the falling particle.\n88\t```\n89\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cf7b2c2c-4ecc-4aff-b38e-3c899e84ab95","uuid":"b927decc-a235-413e-83b2-1b0b5d5d3aa9","timestamp":"2026-06-02T03:49:09.719Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-YM0RJN/project/c_src/README.md","content":"# 2DPartInt\n\nGeotechnical problems have been traditionally tackled as an extension of continuum mechanics theories. Such an approach, although appropriate for systems undergoing small displacements, cannot examine important problems such as landslides, rockfalls, and rainfall-induced landslides. Our implementation is a first step toward analyzing these complex behavior, and, as a starting point, studies systems constituted of geomaterials assumed as particles in a two-dimensional space. With this implementation, we plan to bring to the geotechnical community, academic and practitioners in general, a computational tool that will allow solving challenges involving multiphysics and large displacements effectively and accurately.\n\n![Simulation](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/eafit-apolo/2DPartInt/master/simulacion.gif)\n\n## Usage\n\nCompile the project using make.\n\n```bash\n$ make\n```\n\n_(Optional)_ run the tests to ensure everything is ok.\n\n\n```bash\n$ make test\n```\n\nTo debug some simulation step compile like this.\n\n```bash\n$ make DEBUG_STEP=true\n```\n\nExecute the program passing the `simulation_config.txt` file & the output folder as the arguments.\n\n```bash\n$ ./bin/2DPartInt simulation_config.txt out/\n```\n\n### Profile the program\n\nInstall the package `gperftools` or the equivalent in your operating system.\n\nCompile defining the `PROFILING` flag.\n\n```\n$ make PROFILING=true\n```\n\nRun defining where to store the profiler output.\n\n```\n$ CPUPROFILE=profiler_out.prof ./bin/2DPartInt simulation_config.txt out/\n```\n\nPrint profiling information.\n\n```\n$ pprof --text ./bin/2DPartInt profiler_out.prof\n```\n\nMore runtime and offline options for the profiler [here](https://gperftools.github.io/gperftools/cpuprofile.html).\n\n## Simulation Config File.\n\nThe behaviour of the simulation is determined by the config file. For finding collisions between particles, we use a Grid-like\ndata structure. The area covered by the Grid will be the only area in space that will be simulated. Particles whose center lies\noutside the Grid area will not be taken into account for findind collisions between particles, even though they would mantain\ntheir forces.\n\nIn the config file one defines the number of squares on the X coordinate and the number of squares on the Y coordinate, along\nwith the length of each square. A configuration of x_squares=4, y_squares=3 will yield the following grid:\n\n![OnlyGrid](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/eafit-apolo/2DPartInt/master/img/OnlyGrid.jpg)\n\nThis is the structure of the file.\n_(All settings are mandatory, but they can be in any order). You should not add comentaries as below._\n\n```\ntime=[Double] # Total time to simulate, in seconds.\ndt=[Double] # Size of the time window used for each step.\nx_particles=[Int] # Number of particles along the X coordinate\ny_particles=[Int] # Number of particles along the Y coordinate\nx_squares=[Int] # Number of squares along the X coordinate\ny_squares=[Int] # Number of squares along the X coordinate\nsquare_in_grid_length=[Double] # Length side of each square in the grid\nradius=[Int] # Radius of each particle.\nkn=[Double] # Normal rigidity of the material.\nks=[Double] # Tangential rigidity of the material.\nrho=[Double] # Density of the material.\nthickness=[Double] # Thickness of the particles.\nv0=[Double] # Initial velocity of the falling particle.\nr0=[Double] # Radius of the falling particle.\n```\n","numLines":89,"startLine":1,"totalLines":89}}}
